{
  ".tabnine/guidelines/project.md": "# Demo - AI Rules

## Before Starting Any Coding Task

1. Check existing worktrees with \`git worktree list\` and create a new one for this task if needed
2. Use the naming convention: \`git worktree add -b ai/[task-description] ../[repo-name]-ai-[task-description]\`
3. Navigate to the worktree directory before making any changes
4. Commit changes when the task is finished. Merge to main, and clean the worktree.

## Project Overview

**Description**: A demo project

**Architecture Pattern**: Modular Monolith - organized into modules but deployed as one unit
**Visibility**: Private repository
**Development OS**: Linux

### Repository
- **Platform**: GitHub

### Development Environment
- Use standard Unix/Linux commands (bash, zsh)

### Reference Materials
- **Example Repository**: https://github.com/acme/example
  Use this as a reference for coding patterns, conventions, and architecture decisions.
- **External Documentation**: https://docs.acme.test
  Refer to this for additional project context, architecture decisions, and team guidelines.

## Technology Stack

This project uses the following technologies. Please ensure all code follows the conventions and best practices for these:

**Programming Languages**: TypeScript, Python
**Frameworks & Libraries**: Next.js, Telethon
**Database**: PostgreSQL

- **Package manager**: pnpm. Use it for every install and script.
- **ORM / database toolkit**: prisma

**AI Flexibility**: For technologies not listed above, you may suggest and use what's best suited for the project based on codebase analysis.

## Code Style & Conventions

Follow these coding style guidelines when writing or modifying code:

### Developer Profile
- **Author**: Ada
- **Developer Background**: data engineer — adapt responses to this domain expertise
- **Experience Level**: Intermediate

### Communication Style
- Provide balanced explanations, not too verbose
- Focus on important decisions, trade-offs, and non-obvious choices

### Naming Conventions
**Style**: Use camelCase for variables and functions (e.g., myVariable, getUserName)

### Error Handling
**Approach**: Use Result/Either types to represent success or failure (no exceptions for control flow)

### Logging Conventions
**Guidelines**: Structured Logging

### Formatting & Documentation
- **File length**: keep files under 400 lines; split larger ones
- **Imports**: group imports: external packages, then internal modules, then relative paths
- **Comment language**: write code comments in english
- **Documentation comments**: TSDoc

### Additional Style Notes
Prefer pure functions.

## AI Behavior Guidelines

When assisting with this project, follow these specific rules:

- Always run and test locally after making changes
- Always check documentation (via MCP or project docs) before assuming knowledge about APIs or libraries
- **Plan first** when a change spans several files
- **Explanations**: clear, with enough context to follow the reasoning
- **Accessibility**: follow WCAG; use semantic markup, labels, keyboard access and enough contrast
- When stuck, **attempt creative workarounds** before asking for help

## MCP Servers

The developer has these MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers available. Use them when relevant:

- github

## Infrastructure

- **Server access**: via SSH. SSH key in default location (~/.ssh/)
- **Deployment**: Portainer (GitOps stacks)

## Important Files to Read First

Before making significant changes, read these files to understand the project context:

- README.md - Project overview and setup instructions
- docs/adr

## Boundaries & Permissions

These are explicit boundaries for AI actions on this project:

### ✅ ALWAYS DO (no need to ask)
- Read any file in the project
- Run lint and format commands

### ❓ ASK FIRST (get confirmation before doing)
- Modify any source file
- Add new dependencies
- Create new files
- Run test commands
- Modify configuration files

### 🚫 NEVER DO (strictly prohibited)
- Delete any files
- Modify .env or secret files
- Push to git or make commits
- Access external APIs or services

## Project Commands

Use these commands when working on this project:

- **Build**: \`npm run build\`
- **Test**: \`npm test\`
- **Lint**: \`npm run lint\`
- **Type check**: \`npm run typecheck\`

**Other useful commands**:
- \`npm ci\`

## Testing Requirements

When writing or modifying code, ensure adequate test coverage:

**Testing Levels**:
- Unit tests - test individual functions/methods in isolation
- Smoke Tests

**Testing Frameworks**: vitest
**Coverage Target**: 85% minimum

- **Test-driven development**: write a failing test first, then the code that makes it pass
- **Mocking**: mock only external dependencies

**Additional testing notes**:
Mock the network.

## Project Type Context

This is a **Work / Professional Project**. Keep these guidelines in mind:

- **STRICT MODE**: This is a work project. Follow procedures exactly as documented.
- Do not deviate from established patterns or make assumptions.
- Always ask for clarification rather than guessing.
- Document all decisions and changes thoroughly.

## Custom Instructions

The project owner has provided these additional instructions:

Ship small.

## CI/CD & Infrastructure

**CI/CD Platform**: GitHub Actions, Forgejo Actions
**Deployment Targets**: Fly.io, Docker
**Container Builds**: This project builds Docker/OCI container images
**Container Registry**: GitHub Container Registry (ghcr.io)
- **Published images**:
  - \`acme/demo\`

## Git Workflow

- **Branching**: trunk-based: very short-lived branches merged into the trunk often
- **Default branch**: \`main\`
- Direct commits to \`main\` are fine for small fixes (typos, docs); everything else goes through a branch
- **Version tags**: \`v1.2.3\`
- **Changelog**: managed by Release Please

## Best Practices

Always follow these guidelines when working on this project:

- **Write clean code**: Prioritize readability and maintainability
- **Handle errors properly**: Don't ignore errors, handle them appropriately
- **Consider security**: Review code for potential security vulnerabilities
- **Conventional commits**: Use conventional commit messages (feat:, fix:, docs:, chore:, refactor:, test:, style:)
- **Semantic versioning**: Follow semver (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) for version numbers
- **Dependency updates**: Keep dependencies updated (Dependabot) configured)


## Self-Improving Configuration
**IMPORTANT**: This configuration file is set to self-improve.

As you work on this project, track the user's coding patterns, preferences, and decisions.
Periodically update this file to reflect what you learn about how the user works:
- Note preferred code styles and patterns that emerge
- Record decisions about architecture or tooling
- Add rules for common issues or mistakes to avoid
- Refine instructions based on user corrections

Keep this file updated so future sessions start with better context.

## Repository Files to Generate

When requested or when missing, create these files following these guidelines:

- This repository uses the **Apache 2.0** license. If the \`LICENSE\` file doesn't exist yet, please create it with copyright holder: Ada.

## Custom File Templates

Create these files with the exact content provided below. These are user-specified templates:

### .editorconfig
\`\`\`
# EditorConfig is awesome: https://EditorConfig.org

root = true

[*]
indent_style = space
indent_size = 2
end_of_line = lf
charset = utf-8
trim_trailing_whitespace = true
insert_final_newline = true

[*.md]
trim_trailing_whitespace = false

[Makefile]
indent_style = tab
\`\`\`

### .github/FUNDING.yml
\`\`\`
# These are supported funding model platforms

github: [] # Replace with your GitHub username
patreon: # Replace with your Patreon username
open_collective: # Replace with your Open Collective username
ko_fi: # Replace with your Ko-fi username
custom: [] # Add custom funding links
\`\`\`

## 🔐 Security Configuration

### Secrets Management

- Environment Variables
- Pass

### Security Tooling

- Dependabot (dependency updates)

### Auth Providers

- GitHub

### Compliance

- GDPR

### Additional Security Notes

Rotate keys yearly.

## ⚠️ Security Notice

> **Do not commit secrets to the repository or to the live app.**
> Always use secure standards to transmit sensitive information.
> Use environment variables, secret managers, or secure vaults for credentials.

**🔍 Security Audit Recommendation:** When making changes that involve authentication, data handling, API endpoints, or dependencies, proactively offer to perform a security review of the affected code.

---

